# Target C-II — outer-star train closure gives a `0.7916D` exact-flex product

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only structural strengthening; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** terminal full-core Type-II FREE subsequences in the live window `t_D <= mu_*+0.00710+o(1)`  
**Depends on:** audited complete stopped-train decomposition; strict convexity of bounded contact faces; train-angle closure coordinates; terminal correlated stability; canonical mixed infinitesimal rigidity; C65 relative-interior KKT positivity  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_outer_star_exact_flex_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current live flex theorem extracts one degree of freedom from each fully rhombic
pentagon having at least three outer-reaching trains. That throws away two pieces of
available structure:

1. an outer-reaching train can be used as a train-angle variable even when it is a
   singleton or when its bounded endpoint is not a pentagon;
2. a bounded face with `j>=3` outer-reaching train variables has `j-2`, not merely
   one, local closure degrees of freedom.

Keeping all of those variables removes the singleton branch from the live exact-flex
statement. The endpoint transport multigraph alone forces a simultaneous exact
contact-flex product of dimension

\[
\boxed{
 m_D\ge {b-2f\over2}
}
\tag{OS.1}
\]

throughout the present terminal window (for all sufficiently large members of the
sequence). Consequently, if

\[
 t_D\le\mu_*+0.00710+o(1),
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {m_D\over D}
\ge 0.791663281303730\ldots .
}
\tag{OS.2}
\]

The diameter-locking lemma from the current live theorem therefore gives a matching
from at least `0.7916632813D-o(D)` independent exact contact-flex coordinates to
pairwise distinct actual diameter bars, and at least that many positive and negative
diameter derivative incidences separately.

In the small-complement branch of the latest complement/detour theorem, where the
retained complement O--O pair mass is at most `0.01D+o(D)`, the forced near-maximal
outer detour improves the product floor further to

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {m_D\over D}
\ge 0.817538281303730\ldots .
}
\tag{OS.3}
\]

No rhombic-fullness assumption and no lower bound on an individual KKT multiplier is
used.

---

## 1. Outer-star variables at an arbitrary bounded exceptional face

Use the exact endpoint transport multigraph. Its vertices are the actual outer face
`O` and the `f` bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces; every complete stopped train is
one transport edge. Bounded vertices support no loops. Write

\[
o(F):=\#\{\text{transport edges joining }F\text{ to }O\}.
\]

Thus

\[
\sum_F o(F)=E_{BO},
\qquad
E_{BO}+2L_O=b,
\tag{1.1}
\]

where `L_O` is the number of O--O trains.

Fix one bounded exceptional face `F` with `j=o(F)>=3`. Canonical bounded-face
convexity makes `F` a simple strictly convex unit-edge polygon. Let the oriented side
vectors belonging to its `j` outer-reaching trains be

\[
u_1,\ldots,u_j.
\]

All other train directions incident to `F` are held fixed. Rotating those `j` train
directions gives the exact face-closure map

\[
\Phi_F(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_j)
=
\sum_{i=1}^j R_{\theta_i}u_i+c_F,
\tag{1.2}
\]

where `c_F` is the fixed sum of the remaining oriented sides and `Phi_F(0)=0`.
Its derivative is

\[
D\Phi_F(0)
=
\begin{bmatrix}Ju_1&\cdots&Ju_j\end{bmatrix}.
\tag{1.3}
\]

A strictly convex polygon cannot have three sides in one projective direction.
Therefore among any `j>=3` selected side directions there are two nonparallel ones,
and

\[
\operatorname{rank}D\Phi_F(0)=2.
\tag{1.4}
\]

The implicit-function theorem supplies an exact local closure manifold of dimension

\[
\boxed{j-2.}
\tag{1.5}
\]

This argument does not require the selected train to contain a rhombus. In particular,
a P--O singleton train is a legitimate train-angle variable: it is one contact edge,
it has only `F` as a bounded exceptional endpoint, and rotating its edge vector changes
only the closure equation of `F` among the bounded exceptional faces.

---

## 2. The local manifolds multiply exactly

A P--O train has exactly one bounded endpoint. Hence the outer-reaching train-variable
sets used at two different bounded faces are disjoint. O--O trains occur in no bounded
exceptional-face closure equation at all.

For each bounded face with `o(F)>=3`, take the local manifold from Section 1. Assign
independent small angles to every O--O train. These parameter sets are disjoint, so the
local manifolds and the O--O angle box form a product of dimension

\[
M_D
:=
L_O+\sum_F (o(F)-2)_+.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Every strict rhombus closes identically for arbitrary values of the two train
directions crossing it: its oriented boundary remains `u+v-u-v`. Every bounded
exceptional face closes by construction. Bounded facial cycles generate the cycle
space of the connected plane contact graph, so all graph cycles close. Fixing one core
vertex then integrates the assigned unit edge vectors to a finite contact-preserving
realization.

The construction is on the positive-contact core. Contact-isolated source points can
be kept fixed. Every old noncontact has strict distance greater than one, so for each
finite source one sufficiently small product neighborhood preserves every strict
unit-separation inequality. The contact graph, and hence triangle-freeness, is
unchanged.

In the live terminal window the transport surplus gives

\[
E_{BB}-L_O
\ge
\left(e_*-{5\over2}(0.00710)-o(1)\right)D
=
(0.3237862869\ldots-o(1))D.
\tag{2.2}
\]

Thus a B--B train exists for all sufficiently large members of the sequence. No B--B
train angle is varied in (2.1), whereas a nonzero global rotation changes every train
angle equally. Therefore the product tangent contains no global-rotation direction.
After fixing translation, its non-Euclidean dimension is exactly `M_D`. We write

\[
m_D:=M_D.
\tag{2.3}
\]

Even without (2.2), quotienting by global rotation would lose at most one dimension,
which is asymptotically irrelevant.

---

## 3. Finite transport count: `m_D >= (b-2f)/2`

For every integer `j>=0`,

\[
(j-2)_+\ge j-2.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Hence (1.1)--(2.1) give

\[
\begin{aligned}
m_D
&=L_O+\sum_F(o(F)-2)_+\\
&\ge L_O+E_{BO}-2f\\
&=b-L_O-2f.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.2}
\]

Trivially `m_D>=L_O` as well. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
 m_D
\ge
\max\{L_O,\ b-L_O-2f\}
\ge {b-2f\over2}.
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

This is a finite statement. It uses no pentagon count, no full-flower estimate, no
singleton/rhombic split, and no asymptotic optimization.

The old live theorem used

\[
\max\{S/D,L_O/D,F^{full}_{5,\ge3O}/D\}
\ge0.1407115\ldots-o(1).
\]

Equation (3.3) shows why that trichotomy was unnecessarily lossy for contact-flex
purposes: singleton P--O trains and non-fully-rhombic bounded faces still participate
in the exact face-closure manifold.

---

## 4. Terminal correlated stability gives `0.791663...D`

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
g=0.007707,
\]

\[
M_*=4s_2+2g,
\qquad
p_*={M_*\over5}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

For a terminal subsequence with

\[
\limsup t_D\le\mu_*+\varepsilon,
\]

the exact correlated stability variables satisfy

\[
a+2(r+\delta)\le5\varepsilon,
\qquad a,r,\delta\ge0,
\tag{4.2}
\]

and

\[
{f\over D}
={M_*+2a-\delta-r\over5}+o(1),
\qquad
{b\over D}=\pi+\delta+o(1).
\tag{4.3}
\]

Insert (4.3) into (3.3):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\liminf {m_D\over D}
&\ge
{\pi\over2}-{M_*\over5}
+{7\over10}\delta+{1\over5}r-{2\over5}a\\
&=
{\pi\over2}-p_*
+{7\over10}\delta+{1\over5}r-{2\over5}a.
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.4}
\]

The positive coefficients of `delta` and `r` mean the minimum under (4.2) occurs at

\[
\delta=r=0,
\qquad a=5\varepsilon.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {m_D\over D}
\ge
c_{\rm star}(\varepsilon)
:={\pi\over2}-p_*-2\varepsilon.
}
\tag{4.5}
\]

At

\[
\varepsilon_0=0.00710,
\]

the exact-rational certificate gives

\[
\boxed{c_{\rm star}(\varepsilon_0)
=0.7916632813037307425\ldots .}
\tag{4.6}
\]

This is more than five times the previous live exact-flex guarantee
`0.1407115056...D`.

---

## 5. Near-maximal-detour branch: `0.817538...D`

The latest complement/detour theorem says that if the retained complement O--O pair
mass is at most `0.01D+o(D)`, then in the same `epsilon_0=0.00710` window

\[
\delta>0.01725,
\qquad
a+2r\le0.001,
\tag{5.1}
\]

with `a,r>=0`.

Apply (4.4). For fixed `delta`, the expression is minimized by putting the whole
remaining defect budget into `a`, not `r`. Thus (5.1) gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
\liminf {m_D\over D}
&\ge
{\pi\over2}-p_*
+{7\over10}(0.01725)-{2\over5}(0.001)\\
&=
\boxed{0.8175382813037307425\ldots .}
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.2}
\]

The inequality is strict at the scalar level because `delta>0.01725`; the displayed
constant is a safe closed lower floor.

---

## 6. Diameter-locking consequence

Apply the already proved general diameter-locking lemma to the exact product manifold.
Let `G_D` be the actual-diameter squared-length derivative matrix on any tangent basis
of this product. Mixed infinitesimal rigidity gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}G_D=m_D.
\tag{6.1}
\]

The C65 relative-interior KKT coefficients form a strictly positive dependence among
the rows. Hence the rows positively span `R^{m_D}`. Hall's rank argument supplies a
matching from all `m_D` flex coordinates to `m_D` pairwise distinct actual diameter
bars. Every nonzero column has both derivative signs.

Thus in the full live terminal window,

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{rank}G_D&\ge(0.791663281303730\ldots-o(1))D,\\
|M_{flex\to H}|&\ge(0.791663281303730\ldots-o(1))D,\\
I_+&\ge(0.791663281303730\ldots-o(1))D,\\
I_-&\ge(0.791663281303730\ldots-o(1))D.
\end{aligned}}
\tag{6.2}
\]

In the small-complement/near-maximal-detour branch every `0.791663...` in (6.2) can
be replaced by `0.817538...`.

---

## 7. What remains

This does not close C-II. A positive-spanning active-diameter system is precisely the
first-order KKT obstruction; increasing its rank does not by itself produce a common
diameter-decreasing tangent.

The useful change is that the former singleton alternative is gone from the live
window. Every live terminal survivor now has one large, explicit, finite exact-flex
product built only from O--O trains and bounded-face outer stars. The remaining target
is correspondingly narrower: prove that a straight-line diameter thrackle cannot
positively lock this outer-star product with the required signed support pattern, or
use the exact product to obtain a source-preserving higher-order diameter descent.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** proved the finite outer-star exact-flex product, the transport floor
`m_D >= (b-2f)/2`, the live `0.791663...D` exact-flex/matching floor, and the
near-maximal-detour `0.817538...D` refinement. C-II remains open.
